/T (60, 60, 61, 63, 63, 63, 64, 66, 68, 70, 92 and 99. The class coordinator wants to report a single number that best represents the performance of the class. Write an R program that \(a\) stores the marks in a vector, \(b\) defines a user function get_mode\(\) to find the mode, \(c\) computes the mean, median, mode and the 10% trimmed mean, and \(d\) analyses the gap between the mean and the median to decide which measure is the more reliable representative value. Use the rule: if the absolute difference between the mean and the median exceeds 2 marks, recommend the median; otherwise recommend the mean. Explain how the two exceptionally high scores influence each measure. )
/E (60, 60, 61, 63, 63, 63, 64, 66, 68, 70, 92 and 99. The class coordinator wants to report a single number that best represents the performance of the class. Write an R program that \(a\) stores the marks in a vector, \(b\) defines a user function get_mode\(\) to find the mode, \(c\) computes the mean, median, mode and the 10% trimmed mean, and \(d\) analyses the gap between the mean and the median to decide which measure is the more reliable representative value. Use the rule: if the absolute difference between the mean and the median exceeds 2 marks, recommend the median; otherwise recommend the mean. Explain how the two exceptionally high scores influence each measure. )
