/P 193 0 R
/T (A start-up lists the monthly salaries \(in thousands of rupees\) of its 12 employees as 28, 30, 31, 32, 33, 35, 36, 38, 40, 45, 120 and 150. The HR manager advertises the 'average salary' to new applicants. Evaluate whether this claim is fair. Write an R program that computes the mean, the median and the skewness of the salaries, defining a function skew\(\) based on the third standardised moment. Apply the rule: if the skewness is greater than 1 the distribution is strongly right-skewed and the median should be quoted; otherwise the mean is acceptable. Print the recommended measure, the amount \(and percentage\) by which the mean overstates the median, and a one-line critique of the HR manager's claim. Conclude by stating which measure you would defend before an audit committee. )
/E (A start-up lists the monthly salaries \(in thousands of rupees\) of its 12 employees as 28, 30, 31, 32, 33, 35, 36, 38, 40, 45, 120 and 150. The HR manager advertises the 'average salary' to new applicants. Evaluate whether this claim is fair. Write an R program that computes the mean, the median and the skewness of the salaries, defining a function skew\(\) based on the third standardised moment. Apply the rule: if the skewness is greater than 1 the distribution is strongly right-skewed and the median should be quoted; otherwise the mean is acceptable. Print the recommended measure, the amount \(and percentage\) by which the mean overstates the median, and a one-line critique of the HR manager's claim. Conclude by stating which measure you would defend before an audit committee. )
/Pg 15 0 R
/K [19]
>>
