/T (problems solved \(y\) by ten students: y = 3, 5, 4, 6, 7, 6, 8, 9, 10 and 45. The last value \(45\) is unusually high because that student had solved a problem set in advance. Write an R program that computes the Pearson and Spearman correlation coefficients \(method = "spearman"\) for all ten observations and again after excluding the tenth student, prints the four coefficients, the shift in each coefficient caused by the outlier, and identifies which coefficient is more robust. Evaluate whichmeasure the teacher should report, and explain why a rank-based coefficient is less sensitivetoextreme values. )
